grund check — validate every ID citation across the repo.

Usage:  grund check [PATH] [--watch] [--rule SENTENCE] [--full] [--require-grounding] [--suggestions] [--only CODE] [--ignore CODE] [--only-rule] [--format text|json]

PATH defaults to `.`; config is discovered by walking up from it — the root `grund.toml` is the home, `.agents/grund.toml` a deprecated fallback.
With no config, grund scans `docs/`, `e2e/`, and `src/`; set `[scan] include` to widen it.
Pointing grund at an explicit PATH reports exactly that file or directory; citations still resolve against the whole project.
Path validation is explicit; `grund PATH` is parsed as an ID query.

Options:
  --watch              check immediately, then re-check on native filesystem notifications.
                       Ctrl-C returns the last completed status (2 before completion).
                       Text may use an alternate screen; clean JSON runs emit no record.
  --format text|json   text (default) prints `success` or `path:line: <channel>: message`,
                       grouped errors, warnings, then enabled suggestions; json emits NDJSON.
  --full               also walk past [scan] include and report unresolved references or section headings outside declaration bodies there.
  --require-grounding  also require every source file to cite a declared ID ([reference] require_grounding; a [[kinds]] row that sets it false stays exempt).
  --suggestions        also surface should/should-not citation-direction findings ([citations]).
  --rule <sentence>    add one controlled-English chapter/citation rule to configured rules.
  --only <code>        retain one exact finding code; repeat for a union (`--only=<code>` also works).
  --ignore <code>      remove one exact finding code; repeat for a union; ignore wins over only (`--ignore=<code>` also works).
  --only-rule          retain only what the --rule sentence found; requires --rule.
  --path-base project|invocation
                       project (default) spells report paths from the config root;
                       invocation: relative to the path you passed, or the current directory if you passed none.
                       --path-base=invocation replaces a committed `[output] relative_paths = false`.
                       --format json replaces a committed `[output] format = "json"`; a flag outranks its key.

Findings go to stdout (the linter convention) — `grund check | …` and `grund check
--format json | jq` need no redirect. Only run-level `error:` / `warning:` lines
(unreadable path, empty scan) go to stderr; a clean text run prints `success`.

Selection happens after the complete check. It filters errors, warnings, and enabled
suggestions by exact code; operational failures remain visible and exit 2. Exit 0 means
the selected report has no errors, not that the unselected repository is clean.
Selecting a code a rule produces also selects invalid-rule, so a rule that could not
run is never a pass unless --ignore invalid-rule removes its error.

--only-rule narrows that same report by rule authority instead of by code: only what
the --rule sentence authored, whether it authored a finding alone or jointly with a
declared rule that means the same. The two axes intersect and --ignore wins over both.
Without --rule it is `error: --only-rule requires --rule`, exit 2. A `should`-level
sentence lands in the suggestions channel, so it needs --suggestions too or the scoped
run prints `success`.

Supported check finding codes:
  agents-init
  broken-stub
  chapter-cardinality
  citation-cardinality
  dangling
  declaration-near-miss
  deprecated-config-location
  discouraged-citation
  duplicate
  duplicate-section
  empty-citation-obligation
  empty-scan
  escaped-citation-resolves
  forbidden-citation
  full-scope-ignored
  glob-citation
  inline-citation-style
  invalid-rule
  invalid-value-binding
  invalid-value-declaration
  io
  local-section-citation
  misplaced-declaration
  missing-citation
  missing-index-entry
  missing-section
  missing-snapshot
  nothing-recognized
  optional-member-absent
  orphan-section
  out-of-scope-dangling
  out-of-scope-local-section-citation
  out-of-scope-missing-section
  out-of-scope-shorthand-citation
  out-of-scope-unknown-project
  oversized-lead
  redundant-config
  section-heading-level
  section-outside-declaration
  shorthand-citation
  shorthand-numeric-run
  suggested-citation
  uncited-unit
  ungrounded
  unknown-project
  unlinked-index-entry
  unlisted-workspace-block
  unmarked-heading
  unreached-declaration
  unused
  value-mismatch

Exit:  0 clean · 1 dangling / duplicate / unknown-section / ungrounded findings · 2 unreadable tree or CLI error.

Examples:
  grund check              # check the whole repo
  grund check --watch      # live terminal feedback; Ctrl-C to stop
  grund check docs/        # check one subtree
  grund check --full       # plus unresolved references and outside sections beyond include
  grund check --rule "Each FS should have exactly one requirements chapter." --suggestions
  grund check --ignore agents-init # ask whether the selected content report has errors
  grund check --rule "Each FS must have exactly one security chapter." --only-rule # what does this sentence find?
  grund check --format json | jq # machine-readable diagnostics for CI

Guide:    https://github.com/agent-grounds/grund/blob/main/docs/user-facing/watch.md
Guide:    https://github.com/agent-grounds/grund/blob/main/docs/user-facing/rules.md
Guide:    https://github.com/agent-grounds/grund/blob/main/docs/user-facing/citation-directions.md
Example:  https://github.com/agent-grounds/grund/tree/main/examples/rules
